  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "T.M.Purushothaman vs Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Thursday, The 7Th Day Of January 2016/17Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 1409 of 2006 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 249/2004 of Addl.Sessions Court (Adhoc)-Ii, Alappuzha St 1340/1999 of J.M.F.C.-I, Cherthala Revision Petitioner(S)/Respondent/Accused No.1: ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- T.M.Purushothaman, Chelakkappally House, C.M.C., Ward No.18 Cherthala. By Adv. Sri.N.A.Muraleedharan Respondents/Complainant & State: --------------------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Cherthala Circle, Cherthala. 2. State Of Kerala, Rep. By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. By Public Prosecutor, Shri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 07-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - Crl.R.P. No.1409 of 2006 T.M.Purushothaman vs Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/44469480/ 1 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 7th day of January 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 25.1.1999 at about 2.30 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector of Cherthala Circle purchased 750 gram of peas dhall from the shop of the revision petitioner. The third accused was the firm from which the food items were purchased by the first accused. The second accused was the partner of the third accused. After sampling as per rules, one of the samples was sent to the public analyst for analysis. Ext.P14 report of the public analyst would show that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for peas dhall and therefore, it was adulterated. On the request of the accused, the second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P17 report of the Central Food Laboratory would show that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for peas dhall under Item No.A.18.06.14 of Appendix B of the PFA Rules and therefore, it was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts. P1 to P29 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 was examined and Ext.D1 was marked for the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P17 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P17 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and T.M.Purushothaman vs Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/44469480/ 2 found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P17 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext. P17 report of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and T.M.Purushothaman vs Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/44469480/ 2 found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused."
  },
